      primaryObjective: 'Reconstruct the precise failure mode of the detonation: which EDR rule fired, what specifically triggered it, and whether the actor can iterate around it in their next attempt.',
      clues: [
        { text: 'EDR rule that fired: "Encryptor_Behavior_v3" — a behavioral rule that triggers when a process loads cryptography libraries (BCryptOpenAlgorithmProvider with AES) AND enumerates fixed drives within a 5-second window. The PowerShell process matched both conditions and was hard-killed by the EDR agent in-line. The rule was added to the EDR ruleset 3 months ago after a tabletop exercise. Working as designed.', techniqueId: 'T1486', techniqueName: 'Data Encrypted for Impact' },
        { text: 'Dropper analysis (winlogon_update.exe, captured by EDR before kill): the binary is a custom loader, NOT a known ransomware family\'s standard dropper. SHA256 c3a9…b71f, no VT detections. The binary calls a C2 update-config[.]xyz that returns a small JSON payload specifying which encryptor module to load. The actor designed it to be modular — the encryptor stage is downloaded fresh each time, not bundled.', techniqueId: 'T1105', techniqueName: 'Ingress Tool Transfer' },
        { text: 'UAC bypass evidence: registry modification at HKCU\\Software\\Classes\\ms-settings\\Shell\\Open\\Command — classic fodhelper.exe technique. The dropper successfully elevated to administrator. This means the encryptor would have run with admin rights had it not been killed.', techniqueId: 'T1548.002', techniqueName: 'Bypass User Account Control' },
        { text: 'Why the EDR rule caught it and the actor did NOT design around it: the actor\'s loader is custom but the encryptor module they loaded is an off-the-shelf .NET assembly that uses standard BCrypt APIs. The "Encryptor_Behavior_v3" rule fires on those API calls. If the actor swaps the encryptor module for one that uses non-standard crypto (a CryptoAPI variant, or a custom AES implementation), they could evade this specific rule. The luck is real but specific.', techniqueId: 'T1027', techniqueName: 'Obfuscated Files or Information' },
        { text: 'Initial access vector: winlogon_update.exe was dropped via an ISO-mounted file that the user (Diane Chen, manufacturing line manager) opened from an email attachment at 17:50 yesterday. The dropper sat dormant for almost 9 hours before the actor manually triggered it at 02:47. Hands-on-keyboard timing, not autonomous. The dwell time was very short — this was a smash-and-grab attempt, not a careful supply-chain campaign.', techniqueId: 'T1566.001', techniqueName: 'Spearphishing Attachment' },
      ],
      bossEvent:  null,
      injectIds:  ['LUCKY_COMPLACENCY'],
