      primaryObjective: 'Coordinate the CDN takedown via Cloudflare\'s abuse team, contain the 17 hosts in a coordinated rather than scattered fashion, remove the malicious Edge extension fleet-wide, and write up the SNI-vs-IP detection rule for future cases.',
      clues: [
        { text: 'Cloudflare abuse / takedown coordination: the right path is a formal takedown request via abuse@cloudflare.com (or via their abuse-portal web form) with the attacker domain (telemetry-cdn-update[.]net), evidence of the fronting abuse (the SNI variation test results, the C2 handshake logs from the team\'s own egress), and a request for the customer-account-level action plus the IP-level filtering Cloudflare can perform. Cloudflare\'s SLA on confirmed-abuse takedowns is 24-48 hours. NOT instantaneous — but reliable when the evidence is clean.' },
        { text: 'Cloudflare vendor NPC interaction: Cloudflare\'s abuse-response team responds within 90 minutes with "we can\'t fully scope the campaign and act without a formal takedown request through our published channel — the IOCs you sent in an unstructured email aren\'t actionable by the abuse triage system. Please re-submit via abuse-form.cloudflare.com with the artifacts attached as a single packet." Procedural, not adversarial. The SOC has to handle the bureaucratic discipline of submitting the formal request properly rather than expecting back-channel action.' },
        { text: 'Affected-host containment (phased, not all-at-once): pull the 17 hosts in 3 waves. Wave 1 (4 hosts, lowest-impact users, off-hours): preserve memory, image disk, remove the Edge extension, rotate any credentials observed in the binary\'s outbound payload. Wave 2 (8 hosts during a planned 30-min lunch window): same, faster. Wave 3 (5 hosts, including the higher-impact users, scheduled with their managers): same. Phased rollout means the attacker\'s back-end sees a gradual fade rather than a wall of disappeared hosts at once — preserving the team\'s coordination window.' },
        { text: 'Edge-extension fleet sweep: the malicious extension ID (kdnjehklbgmpklemkncnbidfdmegfaol — "MsEdge Sync Helper") is queried fleet-wide. 23 additional installs surface that haven\'t yet activated their fronted C2 (the malware\'s incubation period is 3-5 weeks; some installs are still inside the dormant window). All 23 are uninstalled proactively; the corresponding endpoints get a precautionary scan but show clean.', techniqueId: 'T1176', techniqueName: 'Browser Extensions' },
        { text: 'Detection rule (new, the durable artifact): "SNI-vs-destination-ASN mismatch" — for any outbound TLS handshake where the SNI hostname\'s ASN-resolution does not match the destination IP\'s ASN-membership (and the destination is NOT in a published-list-of-acceptable-CDNs-for-that-hostname), fire a medium-priority alert. Mature SIEMs can implement this as a streaming detection. The rule generates ~50 alerts per day in a 5,000-employee org — mostly false positives (Microsoft, Google, and Salesforce do use CDNs in non-obvious ways) — but the false-positive list is enumerable and the true positives are valuable enough that the analyst cost is worth it.', techniqueId: 'T1090.003', techniqueName: 'Multi-hop Proxy' },
      ],
