      primaryObjective: 'Coordinate the small need-to-know response, support M&A leadership + Legal in the deal-implications decisions, plan technical containment timed to the M&A team\'s decision on Priya\'s status, and harden DLP for personal-cloud uploads going forward.',
      clues: [
        { text: 'Small need-to-know coordination: the response group is 5 people — the security director (chairs), the M&A team lead (business owner, knows the deal status and the people), the General Counsel (Legal lead on the deal AND on the employment-law angle), the senior HR employee-relations rep, and the SOC lead (technical brief + ongoing factual support). The group meets within 4 hours. The SOC lead briefs the facts factually + without recommendations. The group decides: (a) Priya\'s status (likely: immediate suspension with pay pending investigation, technical access revoked today, not yet terminated), (b) the counterparty engagement question (likely: General Counsel calls Goldsmith\'s General Counsel directly, NOT the deal team — a peer-to-peer Legal-to-Legal conversation outside the deal channel — to confront the leak and assess Goldsmith\'s response), (c) the deal-disclosure question (a complicated one — does the counterparty firm\'s receipt of confidential documents materially change the deal? Does it require disclosure to the company\'s board? Does it affect closing conditions?), (d) the company\'s legal options against Priya (employment + civil + potential criminal — Trade Secrets Act applies on its face).' },
        { text: 'Technical containment timing (synchronized with HR\'s suspension communication): the platform-team system-owner of the M&A document share (Andrea, again — same Andrea from ANALYST-31) is briefed on the need-to-know basis and prepared to execute access revocation. The synchronized action: at the moment HR + Legal communicate the suspension to Priya, Andrea revokes her OneDrive access to the M&A deal folder, her Edge browser policy on corp-laptop-3387 is updated to block ALL personal-cloud destinations (drive.google.com under non-corporate accounts, dropbox.com, icloud.com, etc.) — a tight per-user policy, not yet org-wide — and her corp-laptop is queued for remote retrieval. The window between communication and revocation is approximately 30 seconds, coordinated via a shared bridge.', techniqueId: 'T1078', techniqueName: 'Valid Accounts' },
        { text: 'Counterparty engagement (General Counsel\'s domain, NOT the SOC\'s): the SOC supports by preparing a factual one-pager — the documents that were uploaded, the timeline, the share-permission evidence linking matthew.cole@goldsmith-advisory.com as the recipient — and provides it to the General Counsel for use in his peer-to-peer call with Goldsmith\'s General Counsel. The SOC does NOT make any direct contact with Goldsmith. The peer-to-peer call expectation: Goldsmith\'s GC will (a) acknowledge receipt of the documents, (b) instruct Matthew Cole to delete all copies + sign an attestation, (c) brief his own deal team on a clean-team basis to ensure the leaked information does not influence the negotiation, and (d) potentially face their own internal action against Matthew Cole. None of this is the SOC\'s to drive; the SOC supports with facts.' },
        { text: 'DLP hardening (the durable technical fix): the corporate DLP suite + the corporate-managed browser policy is updated to cover personal-cloud destinations from corporate browsers. Specifically: (a) Edge + Chrome browser policy (delivered via Intune / Group Policy) restricts authentication to drive.google.com, dropbox.com, onedrive.live.com (the personal OneDrive consumer service), icloud.com, and similar to corporate-tenant-only — personal accounts cannot sign in on corporate browsers. (b) Browser-extension telemetry alerts on any sign-in attempt or upload to those destinations under non-corporate identifiers — even though the policy blocks the action, the telemetry captures the attempt as a behavioral signal. (c) The new policy is staged: first deployed to the M&A team (immediate, given the active deal), then to all Finance/Legal/Executive (within 7 days), then org-wide (within 30 days, after a UX review with the broader user base to ensure legitimate personal-cloud use cases — e.g. sharing benefits documents with a spouse — have appropriate exception paths). NOT a blunt overnight org-wide rollout that breaks legitimate use.', techniqueId: 'T1567.002', techniqueName: 'Exfiltration to Cloud Storage' },
        { text: 'Past-pattern hunt (NOT the first time?): the team runs the new personal-cloud-detection signature backward across 90 days of browser-extension telemetry for the entire M&A team (the 8 read-in members). Result: 2 additional team members have made occasional personal-cloud uploads (drive.google.com under personal accounts), but the files involved are minor (1 lunch-receipt scan, 3 personal-photo files — clearly not deal content). The pattern uniquely matches Priya. Priya\'s own history shows the personal-cloud pattern began 8 days ago (same day as the deal-team passing-over) and not before — meaning this is a discrete event tied to the precipitating circumstance, not a long-term insider pattern. Important context for HR/Legal in scoping the response.' },
      ],
      bossEvent:  'During the small need-to-know meeting, the General Counsel asks the SOC lead a sharp question: "Are you ABSOLUTELY certain Matthew Cole has the documents in a way that materially affects the deal — or is it possible the share-permission is sitting on a personal Drive nobody has actually opened? Because the answer changes what I say to Goldsmith\'s GC." This is the kind of question where the SOC has to be calibrated: yes, the share-permission is established (the act-1 evidence is clean); no, the SOC cannot directly prove that Matthew Cole OPENED the documents (without Goldsmith\'s cooperation we cannot see their side\'s access log). The SOC must give the GC the right level of confidence to make HIS decision without overstating. DC 12 Command/Analysis roll: deliver the calibrated answer — "we have confirmed evidence that the share-permission was granted to matthew.cole@goldsmith-advisory.com, we have evidence that an external browser session (NOT Priya\'s) accessed the Drive folder URL containing these documents yesterday, we cannot CONFIRM specific file opens without Goldsmith\'s cooperation, but the technical evidence supports a strong presumption of access." Failure: the SOC either over-claims ("yes he has them, certain") and the GC walks into the Goldsmith call with a claim that gets challenged, OR under-claims ("we cannot say") and the GC walks in with weaker leverage than the evidence actually supports — either way damages the response.',
