    'A sophisticated actor is operating inside the AWS environment using ONLY native cloud APIs — sts:AssumeRole, s3:GetObject, ec2 Describe-*, ssm:GetParameter — with stolen federated credentials. No malware on hosts. No suspicious binaries. No DNS anomalies. The traditional EDR + network-IDS detection stack sees nothing. The ONLY signal: subtle behavioral changes in CloudTrail — a service account that historically only called s3:GetObject on a specific bucket at business hours has, over the past 9 days, started assuming additional roles at 03:00 UTC that it has never touched before. The team has to detect a "no-IOC" intrusion via behavioral baselining, scope an actor whose footprint is invisible to traditional tools, contain by rotating creds + tightening IAM Access Analyzer + deploying SCPs, and brief the CISO on the shift from IOC-based to behavior-based detection in cloud environments.',
  victoryCondition:
    'Detect the cloud-native APT via behavioral baselining of CloudTrail activity (the only available signal), scope the actor\'s full footprint across all assumed roles + assets touched, contain by rotating the compromised federated credentials + revoking active sessions + tightening role-trust + deploying SCPs as guardrails, coordinate with the threat-intel contact for attribution, and brief the CISO honestly on the implications of "no traditional IOC" intrusion patterns — including the forced trade-off when the legitimate automation that depends on those creds is disrupted by the rotation.',
  failureCondition:
    'Try to detect via host-based IOCs (futile — there ARE no host IOCs) and miss the behavioral signal entirely, scope incorrectly (under-scoping misses additional roles; over-scoping rotates dozens of credentials that did not need rotating and breaks production), perform credential rotation without first identifying the legitimate automated job that uses them and pre-arranging its handover, or brief the CISO with overconfident IOC-based language when the institution\'s actual detection capability for this attack class is still maturing.',
