      number:           2,
      seed:             'Behavioral signature confirmed, scope characterized (47 days of customer-events data extracted, 4 roles assumed, careful selective recon), attribution to a known financially-motivated group. Now the containment, with a forced trade-off: rotating the federated credential will break the legitimate nightly ETL pipeline unless the team pre-arranges a clean handover; rotating without handover means tomorrow morning\'s business dashboards do not load.',
      primaryObjective: 'Execute the credential rotation + role-trust tightening + SCP deployment, manage the forced trade-off with the legitimate ETL pipeline owner, deploy durable behavior-based detection, and prepare the CISO brief on the institutional implications.',
      clues: [
        { text: 'The forced trade-off: the legitimate ETL pipeline runs nightly 01:00-04:00 UTC and feeds the business\'s morning operational dashboards (used by ~80 daily users including the CEO). Rotating the data-pipeline-runner credential mid-day means tonight\'s pipeline run is at risk. Two options: (A) coordinate a clean handover with the pipeline owner — issue a NEW federated credential under a new permission set with the same legitimate-scope permissions (NO assume-role on the 4 actor-abused roles), update the Jenkins pipeline config to use the new credential, validate via a manual run, THEN rotate/disable the old credential — ~6 hours of coordinated work, no service impact. (B) Rotate immediately for speed, accept that tonight\'s pipeline does not run, the morning dashboards do not load, and the team apologizes to ~80 users — and to the CEO\'s 8AM standup which depends on those dashboards. Option A is professionally correct; option B is sometimes justifiable but requires deliberate-not-default justification. The team has to choose AND own the choice.', techniqueId: 'T1098.001', techniqueName: 'Additional Cloud Credentials' },
        { text: 'Containment script (the coordinated single-window action, assuming option A): (1) attach an AWSRevokeOlderSessions inline policy to the data-pipeline-runner permission set, killing all current STS tokens. (2) Provision a new permission set "data-pipeline-runner-v2" with the scoped legitimate permissions only (no AssumeRole on the 4 actor-abused roles). (3) Update Jenkins to use v2. (4) Validate v2 via manual pipeline run. (5) Disable the v1 permission set. (6) Revert the trust policy on each of the 4 actor-abused roles to remove data-pipeline-runner from any allow-list (if it was ever there) + add explicit Condition blocks denying the IP ranges associated with the attacker. (7) Deploy two new SCPs: SCP-1 denies sts:AssumeRole where aws:SourceIp is in the known attacker IP ranges from the ISAC IOC packet; SCP-2 denies sts:AssumeRole where the requesting principal is a federated identity AND the requested role is tagged "high-privilege" UNLESS the request carries an aws:MultiFactorAuthAge claim within the past 8 hours (forces re-MFA for high-privilege role assumption). (8) Patch + harden the Jenkins runner (already at-current-patch; remove any unnecessary exposed protocols; rotate the host\'s instance-profile credential as a belt-and-suspenders).', techniqueId: 'T1098', techniqueName: 'Account Manipulation' },
        { text: 'Durable behavior-based detection (the artifact that survives this incident): the SIEM gets new detection rules — (1) for any federated identity, fire an alert if the identity assumes a role that does not appear in its rolling 90-day baseline of assumed roles. (2) For any federated identity, fire an alert if the identity makes API calls outside its rolling 90-day baseline of called services (e.g. a pipeline-runner that has never called organizations:DescribeOrganization suddenly does). (3) For any sourceIPAddress claim associated with a federated credential, fire an alert if the IP is not in the rolling 90-day baseline of source IPs for that credential (e.g. the credential historically only came from us-east-1 EC2 IPs and now comes from a Tor exit). The three rules together specifically detect the "stolen federated credential used from anywhere unusual to do anything unusual" pattern. Each rule generates ~5-15 alerts per day in a 5,000-employee org — mostly false positives (legitimate new automation), but each false-positive cluster is enumerable and the true positives are the high-value ones.', techniqueId: 'T1078.004', techniqueName: 'Cloud Accounts' },
        { text: 'Data-loss measurement (for the CISO + Legal brief): 47 customer-events parquet files extracted, each ~250 MB, each containing approximately one day of customer transactions (~3.2M events per file with names, masked PAN, transaction amounts, merchant categories, timestamps, geo). Total: ~150M customer transactions extracted, covering 47 days of customer activity. The data does NOT include hashed passwords or other authentication material; it DOES include enough transactional pattern data to support downstream fraud (the attacker can synthesize plausible-looking transactions for individual customers based on their pattern, or sell the dataset to a fraud-ring). Reportable; jurisdictional notification analysis pending Legal review.' },
        { text: 'CISO brief preparation: the brief must be candid about an institutional reality — the org\'s detection capability for cloud-native, no-host-IOC intrusions is LESS MATURE than its detection capability for traditional host-based intrusions. The 9-day attacker dwell is partially a reflection of that. The new behavior-based rules close the gap going forward, but the candid framing is "we caught this on the 9th day; an institution with mature cloud-behavior-detection might have caught it on day 1-2; an institution without ANY cloud-behavior-detection would not have caught it for 6+ months." The CISO needs to understand WHY the dwell was longer than the org\'s reputation for fast detection might suggest, and what the new detections do for the next instance.' },
      ],
      bossEvent:  'During the credential-rotation execution (option A), the pipeline owner — Ben, the data-platform engineer who runs the nightly ETL — surfaces a wrinkle the team did not know about: there is a second downstream consumer of the data-pipeline-runner credential, an unofficial scheduled job that one of the data-scientists set up 8 months ago to pull a sample of the data into their personal analysis environment, scheduled to run at 04:30 UTC nightly. The job is not in any IaC, not documented, not known to anyone except the data-scientist (who is currently on vacation). If the team rotates the credential without accounting for the data-scientist\'s job, that job fails silently and the data-scientist\'s ongoing research project is impaired — but the team will not know it failed unless they check, and the data-scientist returns in 4 days. The forced choice is: hold the rotation for 4 days waiting for the data-scientist (unacceptable — actor remains active), proceed with the rotation and let the data-scientist\'s job fail (acceptable IF the team commits to a post-rotation fix-up with the scientist, but requires acknowledging that the org\'s sanctioned-automation tracking is imperfect), or attempt to provision the data-scientist a new credential without their direct input (risky — the team does not fully understand their script\'s permission requirements). DC 12 Vigilance + Command roll to choose option B (proceed with rotation, plan post-rotation fix-up with data-scientist when they return, document the cost of the imperfect-automation-tracking as a lesson) and to communicate the choice transparently to the data platform team. Failure: the team either holds the rotation (the actor continues 4 more days), or fumbles the data-scientist\'s job in a way that creates a permanent rift with the data-platform org and undermines future SOC-engineering cooperation.',
      injectIds:  ['LEGITIMATE_JOB_BREAKS', 'CISO_ASKS_ABOUT_MALWARE'],
